
  [1mError handling.[0m Three real failure modes in a browser-local app, all of which fail silently if ignored:

  - [1mlocalStorage unavailable[0m (private mode, disabled storage) — the app runs in memory and shows a visible banner saying
    changes won't persist. Never pretend a save worked.
  - [1mCorrupt or unparseable stored data[0m — don't wipe it. Keep the bad payload under a backup key, start empty, and tell
    the user. Silently destroying someone's task list is the worst outcome here.
  - [1mStorage quota exceeded[0m — surface the failed write rather than dropping it.

  [1mTesting.[0m Unit tests, per your choice. [38;5;153mdueState.js[39m is where the tests earn their keep, and the cases I'd hold the
  implementation to:

  - overdue vs. due-today boundary — a task due today is [3mnot[0m overdue
  - midnight rollover: the same task, same data, different [38;5;153mtoday[39m → different state
  - [38;5;153mnull[39m due date → [38;5;153mnone[39m, never a crash
  - month and year boundaries (Jan 31 → Feb 1, Dec 31 → Jan 1)
  - the 7-day "soon" edge: day 7 in, day 8 out

  [38;5;153mstore.js[39m gets tests for the corrupt-data and unavailable-storage paths, since those are the ones that destroy data
  when wrong. [38;5;153mrender.js[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m I'd leave to manual checking — DOM tests at this size cost more than they return.

  [1mExplicitly not building[0m (say the word if any of these are wrong): no browser toasts, no service worker, no accounts,
  no event log, no recurring reminders, no stale-task detection, no snooze, no priorities, no tags, no search.
